## 1 · Schema

**Table `users`** — one per aggregate root.

| Column | Type | Nullable | Default | Constraint |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `id` | `uuid` | no | — | PK |
| `email` | `varchar(254)` | no | — | `UNIQUE (email)` — business key |
| `name` | `varchar(120)` | no | — | — |
| `department_id` | `uuid` | yes | `null` | FK → `departments(id)`, `ON DELETE RESTRICT` |
| `version` | `bigint` | no | `0` | optimistic lock |
| `created_at` | `timestamptz` | no | — | filled by the application's `Clock`, not the database |

**Indexes**

| Index | Columns | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `users_pkey` | `id` | PK, created by the constraint |
| `uq_users_email` | `email` | Business uniqueness; also serves the `findByEmail` query |
| `ix_users_department_id` | `department_id` | Foreign key. Without an index, `ON DELETE RESTRICT` does a full scan |

No index on `name`: no query filters by it. An index without a query is write cost with
no return.

**Personal data at rest** — `@.claude/rules/personal-data.md` § At rest. Written for every table
this partial designs, `none` included; a serialized `payload` column is the case schema review
cannot see into, so it is named explicitly with its retention.

| Column | Holds | Retention | Why it is stored at all |
|---|---|---|---|
| `email` | e-mail, personal data | life of the account | Business key of the aggregate; no reduced form can serve a unique lookup by e-mail |
| — | no other column holds personal data | — | — |

**Claim strategy — `outbox_events` only** (omit this block when the case has no outbox). Three
answers, one of them chosen out loud, because the exemplar's unlocked read is correct for
exactly one deployment shape:

| Decision | Value |
|---|---|
| Strategy | Documented single instance — the exemplar's unlocked `SELECT`, no lease column |
| Why | `35-jobs.md` § 3 records **1 replica** by deployment constraint, and its § 6 requires a documented single instance — read from there, not asked here. Stated so the next deployment change has something to contradict |
| What it costs if the constraint breaks | Two instances publish the whole batch twice per pass. The consumer's dedupe absorbs it, and this is the paragraph to revisit before scaling out |
| Who absorbs the duplicate | `processed_events` in this project — or, where the consumer is external, `25-mensageria.md` § 3 names it and says whether the guarantee is contracted, assumed or unknown |

The other two answers are a **lease column** (`claimed_at`, one `UPDATE … WHERE event_id IN
(SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED) RETURNING …`) and **`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` at claim time**
(native query, no extra column). Both are schema or query changes and belong in this partial,
never in a comment in the relay. Whichever is chosen, the gateway method's name says what it
does: `claimPending` that does not claim is renamed or made true.

## 2 · Mapping

Aggregate `User` → entity `UserEntity` in `adapter/out/persistence/user`. Explicit
translation in both directions; neither class knows the other through inheritance.
Every class of this aggregate — entity, enum, Spring Data interface, mapper, adapter —
lives in that one subpackage and stays package-private
(`@.claude/rules/persistence.md` § Boundary).

| Aggregate field | Domain type | Column | Translation |
|---|---|---|---|
| `id` | `UserId` | `id` | `UserId.value()` ↔ `UserId.of(uuid)` |
| `email` | `Email` | `email` | `Email.value()` ↔ `Email.of(text)` |
| `name` | `String` | `name` | direct |
| `departmentId` | `DepartmentId` | `department_id` | `DepartmentId.unassigned()` ↔ `null` in the column |
| — | — | `version` | entity-only. `@Version`, invisible to the domain |

`Email` and `UserId` don't become `@Embeddable`: they wrap a single value and the column
is enough. `@Embeddable` only when the value object has two or more fields that travel
together.

## 3 · Adapter and ports

Each port declared in `10-dominio.md`, and how it's served.

| Port | Signature (from `10-dominio.md`) | Query | Fetch | Index serving it |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `SaveUserPort` | `User save(User user)` | `INSERT` / `UPDATE` by PK | — | `users_pkey` |
| `LoadUserPort` | `Optional<User> byId(UserId id)` | `SELECT` by PK | — | `users_pkey` |
| `UserExistsPort` | `boolean existsByEmail(Email email)` | `SELECT EXISTS` | — | `uq_users_email` |

No port returns a collection in this use case, so there's no pagination or `JOIN FETCH`
to decide. If a listing appears, it's born paginated — rule, § Queries.

**Concurrency:** optimistic locking with `@Version`. Two writes on the same `User` make
the second one fail; the adapter translates the failure into the conflict exception from
`@.claude/rules/error-handling.md`.

## 4 · Migrations

| File | Type | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `V3__create_users.sql` | expand | Creates `users`, the `UNIQUE`, and the FK index |

The SQL lives here, not in a file. This skill never writes under `src/`; the executor
creates the file at the path below from this block, verbatim — re-checking `<N>` against
the migrations on disk at implementation time.

**`src/main/resources/db/migration/V3__create_users.sql`**

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
    id            uuid         NOT NULL,
    email         varchar(254) NOT NULL,
    name          varchar(120) NOT NULL,
    department_id uuid         NULL,
    created_at    timestamptz  NOT NULL,
    version       bigint       NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
    CONSTRAINT pk_users PRIMARY KEY (id),
    CONSTRAINT uq_users_email UNIQUE (email),
    CONSTRAINT fk_users_department FOREIGN KEY (department_id)
        REFERENCES departments (id) ON DELETE RESTRICT
);

CREATE INDEX ix_users_department_id ON users (department_id);
```

New table, so there's no expand/contract pair to schedule. If `users` already existed in
production, the `version` column would enter as `NULL`-allowed in one migration, be
backfilled in another, and only a third release would set it `NOT NULL`.

No earlier migration is edited.

## 5 · Configuration

Values decided for this project. The mandatory ones come from the rule and aren't
re-discussed here — `@.claude/rules/persistence.md` § Configuration.

| Property | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size` | `10` | Twice the container's cores, plus margin. A pool bigger than what the database accepts just moves the queue |
| `spring.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout` | `3000` | Failing fast beats holding the request |
| `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.batch_size` | `50` | There's batch insertion during import; without batching that's 50 round trips |
| `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_inserts` | `true` | Without ordering, the batch breaks on every table change |
| `spring.jpa.open-in-view` | `false` | Rule. Repeated here only because Spring Boot's default is the opposite |

Full shape in `templates/application-persistence.yml.example`.

